#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "27249c57-7f17-5e3e-9a21-0518320f5076" :question/math-academy-id "e-8192" :question/problem "![](images/73/73e2bd9a47c9d16f93d5240db3c4babc36f9fbe703ac9e0af449acaa7528f804.png)\n\nA triangular plate occupies the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) enclosed between the lines ![](images/1f/1fb33239355d26c5ef03e27bcf8b6a9c4c0509d9b045eb92713f0d0256d5e29f.svg) and ![](images/b5/b58ea57e60be24c13d7c381e1323204c3e923297cde1a4c0ba80f7bacf5f08dc.svg) as shown above. The plate's mass density function ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) is given by ![](images/8e/8e97b851d06a96a87df2aff865e5eee8cde69fa8ebb76b314ec691ecae0c6a4c.svg) Find the total mass of the triangular plate." :question/worked-solution "The mass of the triangular plate occupying the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density ![](images/ee/eeea59e41716100d371e35f6a9cb2a2bd7a9285a7b849d8eac060daa0ba4d2be.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/75/7547107cd7e0b796f759b9f61151228a65276fdf9b1ee368f31527f5e88634ee.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/e9/e92fc28ba8f4ba932b8a4596648cf1ad13b47180ee681caad1c72ede2c7fbc68.svg)\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to ![](images/f4/f4e3bd65ea5e543add33f4a03c10e6b51bba71ff45e1eee33a9ce7e01d064ac7.svg) treating ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) as a constant:\n\n![](images/0b/0bd607261ba1e95bff1b7d060d85791252208b530c096dcc2dca555f9a890b25.svg)\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to ![](images/06/06d9e6ee04f202e0e861c51f0cd11ce47727f565d64d834fa625baea04ae11e6.svg)\n\n![](images/59/59ac221987617d4c92321338b3646c155d483ede3f26614a615f6e3e49ff820f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the plate is ![](images/c0/c06c6ac73c4cffd1c6d5f2e1a2a15e193ea80f6a97d595e2e6e9dd478261d2e6.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "d565cc1a-f78f-5712-8a03-df4d4e8f3e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-8193" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\le x \\le 1, \\, 0 \\le y \\le 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x.$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D = \\{ (x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2\\}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) = 4 - 3y^2 + 2x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n= \\int_{0}^2 \\left[ \\int_{0}^1 (4 - 3y^2 + 2x) \\:\\textrm{d}x \\right] \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-3{y}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[4x-3{y}^{2}x+{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4(1)-3{y}^{2}(1)+(1)^{2})-(4(0)-3{y}^{2}(0)-(0)^{2})]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y & =[5y-{y}^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(5(2)-(2)^{3})-(5(0)-(0)^{3}) \\\\  & =(10-8)-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "92b9b5ea-1153-580a-a03a-94338f30fe3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8195" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0db928874dba8d872cde46d1467686010f3484765b2d7e39807b5a32a131b09.png)\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a copper plate that occupies the region $D$ shown above, and has charge density \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y) = \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents one-quarter of the disk of radius $3$ centered at \n$\\left(0,0\\right).$\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y) =  \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\dfrac{4x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}  \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\rho(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ \\rho(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe following equations connect Cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the curve $x^2+y^2=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r]\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\frac{9}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =18[-1-0] \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the quarter-disk is $Q = - 18.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b578e86-a974-5bfa-9f36-df84cb5adca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412213} {:answer/id #uuid "5237d1b0-f1fb-5d1a-9b79-4044e0071da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412219} {:answer/id #uuid "e6913e98-cc07-50d9-911e-709f5cf81f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412236} {:answer/id #uuid "e6aad8ca-6a6b-5d58-b940-699d3f74079d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412237} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c377d6-ca95-51b5-ae36-8e79970e358c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5237d1b0-f1fb-5d1a-9b79-4044e0071da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412219} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6f80eb-f3c5-5713-99fa-3a3e570991ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aeeb285a-1f2b-53b2-a7cc-f9bbdb7632bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-38600" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of the upper half of the elliptical disk $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$ with charge density function $\\rho $ given by $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{y}{1+x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of an upper half-disk $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y\\ge 0}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{y}{1+x}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\,⟹\\,y\\le 2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/a6/a6f503900a79b3d3c9489f20d6e3bbc46fd848b628d5cf43cca99c14279b542f.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the ellipse with the line $y=0.$ So, the $x$-limits are $-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/91/91abb4000ba8b04d5f7125d20f05dea76f985503b859203a0d63ba7d5c89e37d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}[{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}(\\frac{4(1-{x}^{2})}{2}-0)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2(1-x)(1+x)}{1+x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2x)\\,dx & ={[2x-{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =((2-1)-(-2-1)) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the upper-half elliptical disk is $Q=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f30b22a-f94b-5eb0-864f-853a25adc211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412217} {:answer/id #uuid "807db1a0-69e3-5174-9fa5-0c001018905e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412225} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7abadb-9e89-5093-8562-77da6b6eb901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412228} {:answer/id #uuid "fef5f445-e0a2-5e37-8088-8873412a8130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412239} {:answer/id #uuid "ff121484-8b19-5f89-b7ca-d4a9664bbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f30b22a-f94b-5eb0-864f-853a25adc211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412217} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfcd6bae-f1ec-53bf-b79c-1d5fc13d0ef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcb0881c-0114-5bc7-8c87-99ad233f502c" :question/math-academy-id "q-93946" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}48{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}48{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =[16{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =16({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c7f9c8-923a-5abe-a11e-c755601544ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412211} {:answer/id #uuid "0f3a5ebb-d57b-5917-9a5f-4033de233be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412215} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee24f41-091f-515f-a96b-1d49350a8995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412218} {:answer/id #uuid "6c99187c-66f6-5727-b7da-a08c1a5a8fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412221} {:answer/id #uuid "e516b8a4-5922-5a03-bd5b-18b41c484daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c99187c-66f6-5727-b7da-a08c1a5a8fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412221} :answer-field/id #uuid "e78ede4b-d59c-5481-a6c2-71fa96277569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12e862b4-b149-5c0d-8733-0ee2170a820d" :question/math-academy-id "q-93962" :question/problem "Find the mass of a cardboard object which has the form of a quarter-disk, in the second quadrant, of radius $4$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $4$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/79/795390e471ee5e96892dfc979f96b803d4a9a4c267410a3afc60a6672f71e63d.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}2\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [2r]_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (8-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(-(-1)+0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0026a41c-1953-55c7-8abe-d387671576c4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Type I or Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acb8d6d-9a1a-5ed9-a744-33ced2af6c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412212} {:answer/id #uuid "67f78ac5-4224-5bed-8725-7a61dd2bb770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412220} {:answer/id #uuid "6f244892-faf8-54e5-97e0-cc110e7053a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412222} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e9f84e-13bd-5c05-b573-abdb68ec7c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412233} {:answer/id #uuid "d8bab820-110b-56f7-a067-14ea492ee399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f244892-faf8-54e5-97e0-cc110e7053a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412222} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f297c64-0333-52db-87e3-e4390148f8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87aacc28-a327-5afe-ab5f-ccb62ff1e2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94400" :question/problem "Find the total mass of the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x=e,$ above the line $y=1$, with mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "The mass of the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $y=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/d5/d55a7318d1d7259c6cb644e05a234fcb2adfbbf7799371c8e11ece4fb8a7150a.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y-1} & =e \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-limits are $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/2a/2afb5f229d56f8b4777ecfe8c1e6b6ba4b414b909cfb0f509cee3c89dac86a84.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type II region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le e,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{y-1})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(1-(y-1))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{y}-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{y}-1)\\text{d}y & =[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the given region is $M=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129209 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65ae2b65-4d0c-5a25-89f8-e186eda19d33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Over a Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "785b30e5-fdb5-57f3-ae7e-07c4f90828f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412224} {:answer/id #uuid "86ede0b1-0433-531d-b31a-6cf9c4965bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412226} {:answer/id #uuid "88687cee-2381-5540-b5d1-d47f9d40efc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412227} {:answer/id #uuid "a0199975-b22a-5999-abd5-dd985774ca60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412229} {:answer/id #uuid "bec0cf84-db4c-51fd-94e9-2690f758e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec0cf84-db4c-51fd-94e9-2690f758e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412232} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e91776-bec8-51c0-a836-ac39db1991e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3303712-2970-5855-8a2d-a322bb15e1d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94530" :question/problem "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{2}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Mass or Charge Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d26321b-467d-53fc-a13a-de11a8c5f16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412214} {:answer/id #uuid "2a213c44-bf91-56d2-b29b-654d35f0f57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412216} {:answer/id #uuid "769489ae-f985-5b88-9206-bbfa137188c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412223} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f56dc6-6478-5d11-a5c5-cc0916578273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412230} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c7e14a-c307-5ff1-b1d0-65627e8c945f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a213c44-bf91-56d2-b29b-654d35f0f57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412216} :answer-field/id #uuid "089290a5-3a6c-5eae-a2e0-6a3715a9c7b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "474993a9-3dbf-5830-a980-809141314b08" :question/math-academy-id "q-94722" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b984f3689ac5191b10a1305408c4888ddbddd4df600060b8d0a84a8e1559fbe1.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the fourth quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Find the mass of the object." :question/worked-solution "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/f2/f2dc1c7b6e558e0f645ad3b6c3d04182554a5778dd7490103bed6ac2e928bcf3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡r]_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"}]}
